CREATED-BY: DXLog.net v2.6.30
SOAPBOX: First time with Jorge CX6VM and Wilder CX6DRA joining us. They arrived on Wednesday afternoon, while the rest of the team reached the station around noon on Thursday, just in time for the first of many BBQs hosted by Ramon.
SOAPBOX: LU5HM and LW5HR, made two trips to the station beforehand to get everything ready for the contest.
SOAPBOX: However, we decided to bring two Flex radios (courtesy of LW6DG) to use at the in-band stations, which required some additional work inside the shack on Friday.
SOAPBOX: Ramon installed a 60-foot tower 300 meters away from the shack and the main antenna field. The original plan was to use an existing OB17-4, but the tower crew encountered some challenges mounting it on the in-band tower, so we ended up using a KT34 for 20, 15, and 10 meters.
SOAPBOX: This was our first time operating with in-band stations, and we enjoyed it very much.
SOAPBOX: Propagation was not as good as last year for us. Forty meters was particularly challenging on the first night.
SOAPBOX: This was also the first time we had an 80-meter four-square array. It performed great, but there was little activity on the band. Early Sunday morning, 80 meters was almost empty. We kept calling there since 20 meters was closed and continued working JAs until well after sunrise.
SOAPBOX: Congratulations to Krassy, K1LZ, and the team at LZ5R on an amazing score. Watching the scoreboard kept us motivated right through the very last QSO.
SOAPBOX: Thanks to everyone who gave us a call.
SOAPBOX: CU next year.
SOAPBOX: The ops at LP1H
